# Chapter 1 · What Gender Studies Is

> Before you compare Gender Studies with anything, you must be able to say what it is, what it studies, what it wants, and why the world needed it. That is this chapter.

## 1.1  Defining Gender Studies

::: define Gender Studies | the exam definition
Gender Studies is the academic field that studies **how society makes femininity and masculinity**, how this **shares out power, money and chances** between women, men and gender-diverse people, and how gender **mixes with** class, religion, race, age, disability and sexuality. It uses the tools of **many disciplines**.
-> Learn this definition. It holds the four ideas the examiner looks for: society *makes* gender; gender is about *power*; the field uses *many disciplines*; gender *mixes with* other identities.
:::

Each part of the definition can become a heading of an answer:

1. **It is an academic field.** It has departments, degrees, journals and research methods. It is not just a slogan, although it has close links with the women's movement.
2. **It studies how gender is made.** Its starting point is that gender is made by society, not simply given by nature (Chapters 6–10).
3. **It studies power.** It asks who gains and who loses from the way gender is arranged — at home, at work, in politics, in religion and in law.
4. **It studies everyone.** Women, men, and people who do not fit into two boxes — and the *relations* between them.
5. **It uses many disciplines.** Sociology, psychology, anthropology, history, economics, politics, law, biology and literature (Chapter 3).
6. **It sees overlaps.** Gender never works alone. A poor village woman and a rich city woman do not live the same "womanhood". This idea is called **intersectionality**.

::: simple
Gender Studies asks three questions about any part of life: *What do we expect from women and from men here? Where did these expectations come from? Who gains, who pays, and how could it be fairer?*
:::

## 1.2  What Gender Studies studies — its scope

Gender touches every part of life, so the field is wide. Its main areas are:

- **Identity and the body:** how people come to see themselves as women, men or otherwise; sexuality; health and childbirth.
- **Family and home:** marriage, housework, care, inheritance, and violence at home.
- **Work and the economy:** paid and unpaid work, the pay gap, informal work, development.
- **Politics and the state:** voting, representation, law and policy.
- **Culture and knowledge:** religion, language, media, literature and education.
- **Violence and security:** gender-based violence, war and peace-building.
- **Global changes:** colonialism, globalisation, migration and climate change.

::: example | One issue, many angles
Take **child marriage** in Pakistan. Gender Studies asks about *identity* (how girls learn what a "good daughter" is), *family* (why parents marry daughters early — poverty, "honour", dowry), *economy* (the girl's lost schooling and earnings), *law* (the Child Marriage Restraint Act of 1929; Sindh's law of 2013; Islamabad's law of 2025), *religion* (the debate in the Council of Islamic Ideology), *health* (early pregnancy and deaths in childbirth) and *power* (who decides). No older subject asked all these questions together.
:::

## 1.3  The aims of Gender Studies

1. **To make the invisible visible** — to show where women, and gender, had been left out of knowledge.
2. **To explain inequality** — to find its causes, not only describe it.
3. **To question what looks "natural"** — to test whether roles that seem natural are really social and changeable.
4. **To produce better knowledge** — knowledge that includes the experience of half of humanity.
5. **To guide policy** — to give governments, courts and aid agencies the evidence they need.
6. **To empower** — to support the struggle for equality. Joining study with action is called **praxis**.

## 1.4  Why the discipline was needed

The older disciplines had four blind spots. This section answers the 2019 question that asked you to "trace the need for the establishment of Gender Studies as a distinct discipline".

### (1) Knowledge treated men as the normal human

For centuries, knowledge was **androcentric** — it treated men's lives as human lives. History was the story of kings and generals. Medicine tested medicines mostly on men. Psychology built its theories of moral growth by studying boys. When **Carol Gilligan** (*In a Different Voice*, ==1982==) studied girls, she found an "ethic of care" that the old theory had wrongly ranked as weaker.

### (2) Women's work was not counted

::: thinker Marilyn Waring | New Zealand economist and former MP · *If Women Counted* (1988)
Waring showed that the United Nations system for measuring a country's income (GDP) counted a woman's work only when she was paid for it. Cooking, cleaning, caring for children and the old, fetching water and growing food for the family were "not work".
-> Economics had made most of women's labour invisible — so the policies built on it were blind too.
:::

### (3) The women's movement asked questions no one could answer

In the 1960s the women's movement asked: Why do women earn less? Why is violence at home ignored? Why do so few women hold power? The universities had no courses, no data and no theories to answer. **Women's Studies** was created as "the academic arm of the women's movement".

### (4) "Adding women" was not enough

The first step was to "add women and stir" — a chapter on women in a history book, a class on women in a development course. This left the old assumptions untouched. A new field was needed that questioned the frameworks themselves and later studied men and gender relations too. That field became **Gender Studies** (Chapter 2).

::: fig flow | Why a separate discipline was needed: four blind spots produced a new field.
@per 5
Men as the norm: knowledge ignored women
Uncounted work: women's labour invisible
Unanswered questions: raised by the women's movement
"Add women and stir": not enough
Gender Studies: a field that studies gender itself
:::

## 1.5  The main features of the discipline

- **It uses many disciplines, and mixes them** (Chapter 3).
- **It is committed.** It openly aims at justice. Critics call this bias; supporters call it relevance.
- **It questions who makes knowledge.** Feminist researchers try to do research *with* women, not just *on* them.
- **It looks at overlaps.** Since the 1980s it studies how gender mixes with class, race, caste, religion and disability.
- **It compares societies.** Comparison shows that gender differs from place to place — so it is not fixed.

::: thinker Joan Wallach Scott | American historian · "Gender: A Useful Category of Historical Analysis" (1986)
"Gender is a constitutive element of social relationships based on perceived differences between the sexes, and gender is a primary way of signifying relationships of power."
-> In simple words: gender is one of the main ways a society shows and justifies who has power. That is why a whole discipline is built on it.
:::

## 1.6  Why Gender Studies matters — especially for Pakistan

::: pakistan | Why Pakistan needs this discipline
- **International promises:** Pakistan joined **CEDAW** (the UN women's rights treaty) in ==1996== and promised to reach **SDG 5** (gender equality) by ==2030==. Both need gender data, analysis and trained officials.
- **Development:** Pakistan ranked ==143rd of 145== in the Global Gender Gap Report 2026. To close the gap we must understand why only ==24.4%== of working-age women are in the labour force (Labour Force Survey 2024–25) and why women's literacy is only about ==53%== (Census 2023).
- **Law:** many pro-women laws have been passed since 2004. People trained in Gender Studies staff the commissions, protection centres and NGOs that put them into effect.
- **Good government:** gender budgeting and gender-aware planning need the skills this field teaches.
- **Social change:** the field gives society words — "gender", "harassment", "consent", "care work" — to discuss problems that were once hidden at home.
:::

::: eye | The "significance" answer in four headings
When a question asks for "scope and significance", give one heading to each kind of significance: **academic** (complete knowledge; new methods), **policy** (CEDAW, SDGs, gender budgeting), **social** (awareness; changing attitudes) and **Pakistani** (data, laws, institutions). Four headings, each with one piece of evidence, make a full answer to that part.
:::
